# Copy to .env in this folder on the server, once. Owner root, mode 600, never in git.
# The guard keeps a second run from replacing a filled-in file (and its key pair):
#   [ -e .env ] || sudo install -m 600 -o root -g root .env.example .env
# Keep a copy of the filled-in file in the password manager.

# The API key and secret: every token the app makes for a call, every call the
# app makes to LiveKit's API and every webhook LiveKit sends are signed with
# them. Never typed: make-keys.sh makes them on the server, writes them here and
# into ../app/.env (as LIVEKIT_API_KEY and LIVEKIT_API_SECRET), and shows neither.
#   sudo ./make-keys.sh
# After they change, LiveKit has to be recreated to read them:
#   sudo docker compose up -d
LIVEKIT_API_KEY=
LIVEKIT_API_SECRET=

# Where LiveKit sends its webhooks (room started and finished, someone joined or
# left), signed with the key above: the app's public address and the route the
# calls feature adds, https://<the app's public name>/api/calls/livekit/webhook.
# They come back in through Caddy like any other caller's request; LiveKit has no
# other way to the app. While this is empty, make-keys.sh fills it in from
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL in ../app/.env. When the app's address moves, change it here
# too (the old name would answer with a redirect), then `sudo docker compose up -d`.
# To send no webhooks, comment the line out.
LIVEKIT_WEBHOOK_URL=

# --- Egress (recordings and call notes), off until asked for ------------------
# ../README.md step 23, "Egress". Uncomment to start Egress with the rest
# (sudo docker compose up -d); comment it again and
# `sudo docker compose --profile egress rm -sf egress` to stop it.
#COMPOSE_PROFILES=egress

# Where Egress puts files: Storage of this server's Supabase stack, through its
# S3 protocol at the public name, in a private bucket made for it. Never typed:
# make-keys.sh copies the four from ../supabase/stack/.env (S3_PROTOCOL_ACCESS_KEY_ID,
# S3_PROTOCOL_ACCESS_KEY_SECRET, REGION, and SUPABASE_PUBLIC_URL with
# /storage/v1/s3) while they are empty. That key pair is Storage's only S3 key:
# it can read and write every bucket, so this file is as secret as the stack's.
LIVEKIT_EGRESS_S3_ACCESS_KEY=
LIVEKIT_EGRESS_S3_SECRET=
LIVEKIT_EGRESS_S3_REGION=
LIVEKIT_EGRESS_S3_ENDPOINT=
LIVEKIT_EGRESS_BUCKET=team-call-recordings

# --- The call recorder (call notes as people speak), off until asked for -------
# ../README.md step 23, "The call recorder". To start it with the rest, uncomment
# (with egress too, if it runs: COMPOSE_PROFILES=egress,recorder); comment it again and
# `sudo docker compose --profile recorder rm -sf recorder` to stop it. The app takes
# notes through it only once its own switch is on too (TEAM_CALL_RECORDER=on in
# ../app/.env).
#COMPOSE_PROFILES=recorder

# The app's public address, where the recorder asks for its work and sends what it
# heard (in through Caddy, like the webhooks). While empty, make-keys.sh fills it in
# from NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL in ../app/.env.
RECORDER_APP_URL=
# The secret the recorder sends with every request; the same value is
# CAPTURE_FEED_SECRET in ../app/.env. Never typed: make-keys.sh makes it while both are
# empty, or copies the app's here while only this one is, and shows neither.
CAPTURE_FEED_SECRET=
# Deepgram's key, for its streaming API (the recorder's own use of it is billed as
# the app's is). While empty, make-keys.sh copies DEEPGRAM_API_KEY from ../app/.env.
RECORDER_DEEPGRAM_API_KEY=
# How many calls the recorder hears at once (default 10).
RECORDER_MAX_CALLS=

# The server's public IPv4 address, only if LiveKit gets it wrong by itself.
# Empty (the default): LiveKit asks a public STUN server at startup which
# address its packets leave from, which follows the server through a move. Set
# it only if `sudo docker compose logs livekit | grep -m1 -o '"nodeIP": "[^"]*"'`
# shows another address than the server's (../README.md step 23).
LIVEKIT_NODE_IP=
